---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import CourseTimetable from "../components/CourseTimetable.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

// Root-absolute hrefs written by hand in an .astro file skip Astro base
// handling: they work on localhost and 404 on the deployed path. The theme
// components rewrite their own hrefs; an inline link has to do it itself.
const archiveHref = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}archive/`;
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="lede">
    In the first workshop you will put a page through a photocopier ten times and
    watch it become unreadable. Beside it sits a digital copy of the same page,
    made ten times over, byte-for-byte identical to the original.
  </p>

  <p class="lede">
    <strong>Generation loss is not inevitable.</strong> That is an awkward thing
    for a course called Generation Loss to admit in week one, and it is where the
    real question starts: which processes change a copy, what traces those
    changes leave, and when a trace is good enough evidence to say where
    something came from.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Every Wednesday you work on a specimen. You measure what changed, you argue
    about what the change proves, and you deposit the result in the
    <a href={archiveHref}>Class Copy Archive</a> for a later week to use.
  </p>
  <p>
    By week six you will reconstruct a family tree of hand-copied texts from the
    mistakes they share, and name the version nobody in the room has seen — then
    find out where you were wrong. After that the course tests the method until
    it breaks: on a replication model, on error-correcting codes, on a
    restoration that invents detail, and on a provenance record with a hole in it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Scribal error, perceptual audio coding and recursive resampling are three
    different tests of the same method, not three examples of one mechanism. The
    course is careful about that difference, and so is the marking.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Postgraduate students interested in archives, media and evidence. You need to
    be comfortable reading an argument and using a spreadsheet. No Latin, no
    virology, no programming and no information theory are assumed — each is
    introduced where the course needs it, with worked numbers.
  </p>
  <p>
    A laptop and the files on these pages are all you need. Nothing in the core
    course requires equipment, paid software, a cloud account or code you write
    yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>How it runs</h2>
  <p>
    One-hour lecture Mondays, two-hour workshop Wednesdays, twelve weeks with a
    break after week six. About eight hours a week including preparation and
    assessment work. Four assessments: an investigation you design, a
    reconstruction from evidence, a preservation argument you have to cost, and a
    five-minute evidence question at the start of every workshop.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every assessment here can be answered with <em>the evidence does not settle
    this</em>, where the evidence genuinely does not settle it. Saying so well is
    worth full marks.
  </p>

  <h2>The timetable</h2>
  <p>
    Every week pairs a Monday lecture with a Wednesday workshop and a five-minute
    Generation Check at its start. An assessment's own due date can land after
    the week it was introduced — worked practice keeps going in the weeks
    between — so the table below places each one on the week it actually falls
    due.
  </p>
  <CourseTimetable />

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The twelve weeks, what you investigate in each, and what it deposits.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>The four pieces, what they ask, how they are marked and when they are due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Archive" href="/archive/">
      <p>The specimens and model studies the whole course argues from.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches it, and what they disagree about.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
</style>
